Custom Exception
----------------

A custom exception is a user-defined exception that extends the Exception class

        class InsufficientBalanceException extends Exception {

            public InsufficientBalanceException(String message) {
                super(message);
            }
        }

extends Exception → Makes our class a checked exception.
extends RuntimeException → Makes our class an unchecked exception.
Constructor → Accepts an error message.
super(message) → Passes that message to the parent Exception class

why super(message) is used in the constructor of a custom exception class:
Java's parent class already provides functionality to store and retrieve error messages.

A constructor can throw both checked and unchecked exceptions.


        class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {

            InvalidAgeException(String message) {
                super(message);
            }
        }

        public class Main {
            public static void main(String[] args) {

                try {
                    throw new InvalidAgeException("Age must be 18 or above");
                }
                catch (InvalidAgeException e) {
                    System.out.println(e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

What happened?
1. We created an exception with a message.
2. super(message) passed that message to the parent Exception constructor.
3. The parent stored the message.
4. getMessage() retrieved it.